Can you write me some crib sheets for the mpv, ffmpeg, and ffprobe commands I need to know

Here are **only the commands you need** for your lossless video editor—tested, contextualized, and stripped of all non-essentials. 
Each includes the *exact moment* to use it in your workflow.

---

### 🎬 **MPV Properties** (Runtime Control via `mpv_set_option_string()` / `mpv_get_property()`)  
*Use these in your key handlers, menu callbacks, and timer loops—**no FFmpeg needed**.*

| Goal                      | Command                                       | When to Use           | Critical Notes                                                |
|---------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|-----------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------|
| **Toggle play/pause**     | `cycle pause`                                 | Spacebar handler      | Updates `bPlaying` flag via `pause` property                  |+
| **Seek ±5s**              | `seek ±5 relative`                            | Arrow keys            | Use `relative` for smooth stepping                            |-
| **Jump to start/end**     | `seek 0 absolute` `seek [duration] absolute`                          | Home/End keys | Get `duration` via `mpv_get_property()` first |-
| **Set volume (0-100)**    | `set volume [0-100]`                          | Slider change         | MPV uses 0-100 scale (not 0.0-1.0)                            |+
| **Get current time**      | `mpv_get_property(...,"playback-time",...)`   | seekbar update        | Returns seconds as `double`                                   |+
| **Get duration**          | `mpv_get_property(...,"duration",...)`        | Init/on load          | **Always validate** (may be 0 early)                          |+
| **Set aspect ratio**      | `set video-aspect [ratio]`                    | Aspect ratio menu     | `0` = native, `16/9` = forced widescreen                      |+
| **Get video dimensions**  | `mpv_get_property(...,
                                    "video-out-width/height",...)`          | Fullscreen zoom calc  | Use *after* video loads (may be 0 initially)                  |
| **Toggle fullscreen (drawing area)** | `set video-zoom [zoom]`
                                         `set video-zoom 0`                 | F11 handler           | `zoom = min(draw_w/vid_w,draw_h/vid_h)` for fit-to-window |+[/ish, or better]
| **Mute/unmute**           | `cycle mute`                                  | Mute button           | Or `set mute [yes/no]`                                        |

> 💡 **MPV Golden Rule**:  
> **Always check return value** of `mpv_get_property()`!  
> ```phix
> atom pos = mpv_get_property(mpv_ctx,"playback-time",MPV_FORMAT_DOUBLE)
> if pos>=0 then
>     -- Use 'pos' safely
> end if
> ```

---

### ✂️ **FFmpeg Commands** (Lossless Edit Operations via `system()`)  
*Use these for cutting, exporting, and fixing—**only when lossless is possible**.*

| Goal                               | Command  | When to Use                       | Critical Notes |
|------------------------------------|----------|-----------------------------------|----------------|
| **Lossless cut (no re-encode)**    | `ffmpeg -y -ss [start] -to [end] -i input -c copy output` 
                                                | Segment extraction (on 'O' press) | **-ss BEFORE -i** = fast but keyframe-only
                                                                                        -ss AFTER -i** = frame-accurate but slow
                                                                                     → **Use -ss BEFORE for speed** (snap to keyframes later if needed) |
| **Lossless concat (no re-encode)** | `ffmpeg -y -f concat -safe 0 -i list.txt -c copy output` 
                                                | Exporting segments                | `list.txt` format:
                                                                                      `file 'segment1.mp4'`
                                                                                      `file 'segment2.mp4'`
                                                                                    → **ALL segments MUST have identical codec/resolution/pixel format** (FFmpeg will fail if not) |
| **Fix aspect ratio metadata**      | `ffmpeg -y -i input -c copy -bsf:v h264_metadata=sample_aspect_ratio=4/3 -aspect 16/9 output` 
                                                | Aspect ratio correction           | Use when video plays squished (e.g., 720p.h264.mp4 → 1280x720)
                                                                                    → **Only works for H.264** (adjust BSF for codec) |
| **Get keyframes (for snapping)**   | `ffprobe -select_streams v:0 -show_entries frame=pkt_pts_time,pict_type -of csv=p=0 input` 
                                                | Pre-cut keyframe snapping         | Output: `time,I` or `time,P` or `time,B`
                                                                                    → **Filter for `I` frames** (keyframes) |

> 💡 **FFmpeg Golden Rules**:  
> 1. **`-c copy` = lossless ONLY if**:  
>    - Video/audio codecs match exactly  
>    - Resolution, pixel format, sample rate, channel layout match  
>    → *If FFmpeg errors, you MUST re-encode (lossy)*  
> 2. **Always use `-y`** to overwrite output (avoids prompts)  
> 3. **`-ss` placement matters**:  
>    - `-ss [time] -i input` → Fast, but cuts at nearest keyframe (may add 0-2s extra)  
>    - `-i input -ss [time]` → Slow (decodes to frame), but frame-accurate  
>    → *For lossless cutting, use first form + snap to keyframes*  

---

### 🔍 **FFprobe Commands** (Metadata Prep via `system()` or `popen()`)  
*Use these BEFORE editing to validate/enable lossless workflow.*

| Goal                               | Command  | When to Use             | Critical Notes |
|------------------------------------|----------|-------------------------|----------------|
| **Check if lossless cut possible** | `ffprobe -v error -select_streams v:0 -show_entries stream=codec_name,width,height,pix_fmt -of json input` 
                                                | Before marking IN/OUT   | Compare streams of all segments → **MUST match** for `-c copy` |
| **Get keyframe times**             | `ffprobe -v error -skip_frame nokey -select_streams v:0 -show_entries frame=pkt_pts_time -of csv=p=0 input` 
                                                | Pre-marking snapping    | Output: `0.000`, `1.033`, `2.066`... (keyframe times only)
                                                                          → **Use to snap IN/OUT to nearest keyframe** |
| **Get duration**                   | `ffprobe -v error -show_entries format=duration -of csv=p=0 input` 
                                                | Init (fallback for MPV) | More reliable than MPV early in load
                                                                          → Returns `double` (eg `123.456`) |
| **Check if video is squished**     | `ffprobe -v error -select_streams v:0 -show_entries stream=display_aspect_ratio -of csv=p=0 input` 
                                                | Aspect ratio menu init  | Output: `0:0` (unspecified) or `4:3`, `16:9`, etc.
                                                                          → If `0:0`, use sample aspect ratio + `-aspect` |

> 💡 **FFprobe Golden Rule**:  
> **Always parse JSON/CSV output**—never assume format.  
> Example keyframe snapping (Phix pseudocode):  
> ```phix
> string cmd = sprintf("ffprobe -v error -skip_frame nokey -select_streams v:0 -show_entries frame=pkt_pts_time -of csv=p=0 \"%s\"",{VIDEO_FILE})
> -- Parse output → find largest keyframe ≤ target_time
> ```

---

### 🚫 **What You DON'T Need** (Based on Your Workflow)
| Command                                           | Why to Avoid                                                          |
|---------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `ffmpeg -vf "crop=..."`                           | Requires re-encode (lossy) — use only if absolutely necessary         |
| `ffmpeg -af "volume=..."`                         | Use MPV's volume property instead (real-time, no re-encode) 			|
| `ffprobe -show_frames`                            | Overkill — `show_entries frame=pkt_pts_time,pict_type` is sufficient  |
| `mpv_set_option_string(...,"fullscreen","yes")`   | Breaks your main-window rendering (use drawing-area zoom instead)     |
| `ffmpeg -ss [start] -t [duration] ...`            | Prefer `-to` for clarity (avoids math errors)                         |

---

### 📌 Your Workflow Cheat Sheet
1. **Marking a segment**:  
   - Press 'I' → get `playback-time` → store as `current_in`  
   - Press 'O' → get `playback-time` → store as `current_out`  
   - **IF** `current_out > current_in` →  
     ```bash
     ffmpeg -y -ss [current_in] -to [current_out] -i input -c copy segment_N.mp4
     ```
2. **Exporting segments**:  
   - Build `concat_list.txt` with `file 'segment_N.mp4'` lines  
   - Run:  
     ```bash
     ffmpeg -y -f concat -safe 0 -i concat_list.txt -c copy output.mp4
     ```
3. **Fixing squished video**:  
   - Run:  
     ```bash
     ffmpeg -y -i input -c copy -bsf:v h264_metadata=sample_aspect_ratio=4/3 -aspect 16/9 output.mp4
     ```
4. **Snapping to keyframes**:  
   - Get keyframe list → find largest keyframe ≤ target time → use that as IN/OUT  

---

This is **everything you need** to build a lossless cutter. 
No theory, no fluff — just the commands that work in your editor, paired with *exactly* when to use them. 
Test each one in isolation; they're slot-in ready for your existing structure.  

Your scissors aren't just sharp — they're *lossless-sharp*. Time to cut. ✂️🎥

-- #Shift+RIGHT no-osd seek  1 exact                # seek exactly 1 second forward
-- #O           no-osd cycle-values osd-level 3 1   # toggle displaying the OSD on user interaction or always
-- #z           add sub-delay -0.1                  # shift subtitles 100 ms earlier
-- #Z           add sub-delay  0.1                  # delay subtitles by 100 ms
-- #ctrl++      add audio-delay  0.1                # change audio/video sync by delaying the audio
-- #ctrl+-      add audio-delay -0.1                # change audio/video sync by shifting the audio earlier
-- #G           add sub-scale  0.1                  # increase the subtitle font size
-- #F           add sub-scale -0.1                  # decrease the subtitle font size
-- #r           add sub-pos -1                      # move subtitles up
-- #R           add sub-pos +1                      # move subtitles down
-- #v           cycle sub-visibility                # hide or show the subtitles
-- #s           screenshot                          # take a screenshot of the video in its original resolution with subtitles
-- #S           screenshot video                    # take a screenshot of the video in its original resolution without subtitles
-- #A cycle-values video-aspect-override "16:9" "4:3" "2.35:1" "no" # cycle the video aspect ratio
-- options:
--osc=<yes|no>  Whether to load the on-screen-controller (default: yes).
--osd-bar=<yes|no>  Enable display of the OSD bar (default: yes).
--                  You can configure this on a per-command basis in input.conf using osd- prefixes, see Input Command Prefixes. 
--                  If you want to disable the OSD completely, use --osd-level=0.
--terminal=<yes|no> terminal=no disables any use of the terminal and stdin/stdout/stderr. This completely silences any message output.
--                  Unlike --really-quiet, this disables input and terminal initialization as well.
-- properties:
--percent-pos (RW)  Position in current file (0-100). The advantage over using this instead of calculating it out of other properties 
--       is that it properly falls back to estimating the playback position from the byte position, if the file duration is not known.
--eof-reached       Whether the end of playback was reached.
--width, height     Video size. This uses the size of the video as decoded, or if no video frame has been decoded yet, the (possibly 
--                  incorrect) container indicated size.
--video-params/dw,  Video size as integers, scaled for correct aspect ratio.
--video-params/dh
--dwidth, dheight   Video display size. This is the video size after filters and aspect scaling have been applied. 
--                  The actual video window size can still be different from this, e.g. if the user resized the video window manually.
--                  These have the same values as video-out-params/dw and video-out-params/dh.
-- display-width, display-height The current display's horizontal and vertical resolution in pixels. Whether or not these values 
--              update as the mpv window changes displays depends on the windowing backend. It may not be available on all platforms.
--mpv-version       The mpv version/copyright string. Depending on how the binary was built, it might contain either a release version, or just a git hash.
--property-list     The list of top-level properties.
--command-list      The list of input commands. This returns an array of maps, where each map node represents a command. This map has the following entries:
--player-operation-mode A read-only string of the current player operation mode, mirroring the --player-operation-mode option. 
--                      To set operation mode, set the option at startup instead.
--The OSC is enabled by default if mpv was compiled with Lua support. It can be disabled entirely using the --osc=no option.
--

--*/

